🚀 1. What is Caching in Spring Boot

Caching means storing the result of a method call (like a DB query) so the next call with the same parameters is faster because it returns the stored result instead of running the expensive operation again.

Spring Boot provides annotation-based caching using the Spring Cache Abstraction.

⚙️ 2. Steps to Enable Caching
🔸 Step 1: Add Dependency

If you’re using Maven, add Spring Boot Starter Cache:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
</dependency>


For a simple in-memory cache, no extra library is needed.
For production, you can use EhCache, Redis, Caffeine, etc.

👉 Example for Redis cache:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
</dependency>

🔸 Step 2: Enable Caching in Your Application

Add @EnableCaching to your main Spring Boot class:

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.EnableCaching;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching       // 🔥 Enables Spring’s annotation-based caching
public class MyApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
    }
}

🔸 Step 3: Add Caching to Your Service Methods

Use the @Cacheable annotation on the method whose result you want to cache.

import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ProductService {

    @Cacheable("products")    // 🔥 Caches the result using the key = method arguments
    public Product getProductById(Long id) {
        System.out.println("Fetching from DB...");
        // Simulate DB call
        return new Product(id, "Laptop", 1500.0);
    }
}


👉 Explanation:

"products" is the cache name.

The first time the method is called, the result is fetched from the DB and stored in the cache.

The next time with the same ID, it will return from the cache (no DB call).

🔸 Step 4: Clear or Update Cache

Use @CacheEvict to remove data from cache when data changes.

@CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#id")
public void deleteProduct(Long id) {
    System.out.println("Deleting product from DB and cache...");
    // delete from DB
}


Use @CachePut if you want to update the cache whenever the method is called:

@CachePut(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
public Product updateProduct(Product product) {
    System.out.println("Updating DB and cache...");
    return product;
}

🔥 3. Properties (Optional)

You can customize cache behavior in application.properties:

spring.cache.type=simple           # In-memory cache (default)
# For Redis:
# spring.cache.type=redis
# spring.redis.host=localhost
# spring.redis.port=6379

⚡ 4. Key Annotations

| Annotation       | Purpose                                             |
| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| `@Cacheable`     | Stores the result in cache (if not already cached). |
| `@CacheEvict`    | Removes data from cache.                            |
| `@CachePut`      | Updates both cache and DB every time.               |
| `@EnableCaching` | Enables Spring’s caching feature.                   |

📂 5. Example Flow

Call getProductById(1) → goes to DB → stores in cache → returns data.

Call getProductById(1) again → returns from cache (fast, no DB hit).

If product is deleted → @CacheEvict clears the cached entry.

⚡ Quick Tips

For small projects or testing, use SimpleCacheManager (in-memory).

For scalable production, use Redis or EhCache.

Always evict or update cache when the underlying data changes.

Use meaningful cache names and keys.